      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Hey there! First off, congratulations on finishing the rumenotomy—that's a big deal, and it's totally normal to feel a bit of that nervous flutter in your gut, especially when you're worried about invisible threats. You're absolutely right to be vigilant about those nasty things you can't see right away, like internal abscesses or deep-seated infections that start from the inside out.\n\nHere is a quick checklist to keep an eye on, but remember, a good vet is always the one to call if you see anything weird, because they have the best tools to spot those sneaky problems.\n\n**The Infection Watchlist:**\n*   **The Wound:** Keep a close eye on the incision site. If it looks red, hot, or starts oozing pus (which is a sign of infection), that's a red flag. Also, check for any \"sloshing\" sounds when you listen to the abdomen; that can mean fluid is building up inside, which is a bad sign.\n*   **The Gut Sounds:** Listen for rumbling. If the rumen isn't making those familiar gurgles, or if it sounds like a goblin's stomach after a feast (",
